# Shared scan-set contract for the cross-reference gate.
#
# READ BY BOTH scripts/lint-references.sh AND scripts/lint-references.ps1. It is the
# one enumeration definition they consume, and it exists because they did not have
# one: the shell side swept `find src tests sdd scripts packaging gtktest data .
# -maxdepth 4`, where the bare `.` made check 6b repo-wide, while the PowerShell side
# built its set from seven named directories plus four named files. `.agents/`,
# `docs/` and `THIRD-PARTY-LICENSES.md` were therefore linted on Linux and invisible
# on Windows, so a dangling link in any of them failed one gate and passed the other.
# POLICY claimed at the time that neither script could "drift into being the lenient
# one"; the shared corpus pinned only the check-1 regex, and enumeration is where they
# had already drifted.
#
# ONE SET, CONSUMED BY EVERY CHECK. `root` and `file` together define a single
# enumeration, and each port produces it exactly once: `--list-scan`/`-ListScan` prints
# that set, and every check narrows it rather than walking the tree again. A check that
# walks for itself is a second enumeration, and there is no such thing as a harmless
# one — three of them coexisted here and disagreed with each other. What each check
# narrows to is its own business and is documented at the check: 6a scans the whole set,
# 6b its `.md` members, check 1 everything but the register and the two lint scripts,
# checks 2 and 5 the `src/` members.
#
# AND THE ARTIFACT MUST BE READABLE. `--list-scan`/`-ListScan` is the only proof the two
# ports agree — no test can run both, since neither platform has the other's shell — so
# the clean state has to be an EMPTY diff, not a diff a human has to interpret. Both
# ports therefore sort by ORDINAL byte value (`LC_ALL=C sort -u` / an ordinal
# `SortedSet`). Before that, both enumerated the same 235 files and the diff still showed
# fifteen changed lines, purely because one collated case-insensitively and the other by
# locale. A parity proof whose clean state is noisy trains its reader to skim it.
#
# THREE CLASSES OF ENTRY, and the differences matter:
#
#   root / file — content this repository is responsible for.
#
#   exclude     — a repo-relative path PREFIX removed from both halves. These are
#                 GENERATED or VENDORED trees, and the reason to exclude them is not
#                 tidiness: every one of them is gitignored, so whether it is present
#                 depends on what the developer happened to run locally. Sweeping
#                 them makes the gate's verdict depend on the machine rather than on
#                 the commit — `docs/` holds QA review artifacts, `.agents/skills`
#                 holds installed third-party skill documents whose links this
#                 project does not own, and `tests/reports/` holds manual-test output
#                 absent on a fresh clone.
#
#   prescriptive — a LABEL ON A SET MEMBER: a document a reader could ACT on, scanned by
#                 check 5b for prose that instructs the very attribute check 5 rejects.
#                 It is a label rather than a reuse of `file` because the two answer
#                 different questions: `file` means "this repository is responsible for
#                 the links in it", `prescriptive` means "a developer obeys what it
#                 says". Both ports REFUSE TO RUN if a path marked here is not in the
#                 scan set. That check is the difference between a label and a fourth
#                 enumeration: without it, 5b read a list that was neither the bounded
#                 nor the unbounded set, so `--list-scan` did not print what 5b consumed
#                 and the parity artifact certified one of three enumerations the gate
#                 actually used.
#                 `sdd/ANTI-PATTERNS.md` is emphatically in the `file` set and not this
#                 one — the register DESCRIBES the banned attribute (it is an entry
#                 title) and scanning it produced thirteen failures on correct text.
#                 A gate that cries wolf on correct text is a gate someone deletes.
#                 This class lives here, rather than as a literal list inside each
#                 script, for the reason the whole file exists: two hard-coded lists
#                 are two lists that drift, and check 5b would then be stricter on one
#                 platform exactly as check 6 once was.
#
# Deliberately NOT a `git ls-files` enumeration: a tracked-only list silently skips a
# document that has been written but not yet added, which is exactly when its links
# are least likely to have been checked.
#
# SYMLINKS ARE EXCLUDED — and the mechanism is not the obvious one.
#
# A symlinked path must never enter the scan set, because the two platforms do not
# read the same bytes through one. Measured, not assumed, on
# tests/fixtures/escapes-via-symlink.md (git mode 120000 -> ../other-fixtures/
# outside-doc.md): on Linux, a symlink handed to `grep` AS AN EXPLICIT PATH ARGUMENT
# is FOLLOWED — it reads the 12-line target, so the target is scanned twice under two
# names and any dangler inside it is reported twice. Note the qualifier: `grep -r`
# SKIPS symlinks encountered during directory traversal, as does `find -type f`, so
# today's Linux enumeration never reaches that case and the exclusion below is a
# deliberate no-op here. Anyone re-testing this with `grep -r` alone will see nothing
# and wrongly conclude the rule is unnecessary; the two behaviours are different and
# both were measured. On a Windows checkout with core.symlinks=false git materialises
# the link as an ordinary 32-byte text file whose entire content is the target PATH,
# so the gate lints a string nobody authored — no symlink semantics are involved at
# all, which is why no filesystem test can catch it there. Same commit, same path,
# different content, and the platform that reads the wrong bytes is the one whose
# enumeration cannot tell it is doing so. Excluding the link
# costs no coverage at all — the target is independently in the set via its own root
# (tests/other-fixtures/outside-doc.md) — and that, not tidiness, is why this is safe.
#
# The mechanism must be the git INDEX MODE (120000), not a filesystem test, and this
# is the trap: each platform's filesystem test is blind in a different way. `find
# -type l` sees the link on Linux and nothing on a core.symlinks=false checkout, where
# LinkType is empty and the attributes are a plain file — so porting `-type l` to its
# natural Windows equivalent (.LinkType / ReparsePoint / ResolveLinkTarget) excludes
# NOTHING and the two gates still disagree, silently, while reading as parity. The
# index mode is the only property both platforms agree on, because it is git's record
# rather than the OS's. Test BOTH (filesystem OR index): each covers the other's blind
# spot, and the union degrades to "include" only when both are blind, which is the
# safe direction — it can never silently skip an authored document, preserving the
# rule stated immediately above.
#
# Consequence to accept knowingly: the symlink CLASSIFICATION consults git, though the
# ENUMERATION still does not. Outside a git checkout (an exported tarball) the index
# half is unavailable and only the filesystem half applies; that degrades to today's
# Linux behaviour rather than to a wrong answer. We do not run the gate that way.
#
# There is deliberately no `symlink` FIELD below: the rule takes no value, so a field
# would be a constant that both validators must learn for no gain. It is pinned by
# `--self-test`/`-SelfTest` instead, which asserts that every mode-120000 path is
# absent from the scan set AND that at least one such path exists — the second half
# matters, because if the fixture is ever deleted the first half starts passing
# vacuously, which is the exact defect this project already paid for once.
#
# KNOW WHAT THAT ASSERTION DOES NOT PROVE ON LINUX. It tests the OUTCOME (no symlink
# in the set), not the MECHANISM, and on Linux `find -type f` already produces that
# outcome by itself. Verified by mutation: neutering the exclusion here and re-running
# `--self-test` still PASSES; it only fails if the enumeration is ALSO broadened to
# `\( -type f -o -type l \)`. So on this platform the exclusion is unfalsifiable dead
# code and could be deleted with every gate staying green. That is not a defect in the
# assertion — asserting the outcome is right, because Windows reaches the same outcome
# ONLY via this mechanism, which is where it is load-bearing and where the mutation
# does fail. It is a warning: do not read a green Linux self-test as evidence that
# this code works, and do not "simplify it away" on the strength of one.
#
# THE EXCLUSION NOW REACHES EVERY CHECK, and it did not always. Checks 1 and 5 used to
# draw from their own unfiltered walk, and the asymmetry was written down here as
# intended — safe, and argued: check 5 hunts a Rust construct while the only symlink is
# `.md`, and check 1 hunts ISSUES references while the fixture's content is a bare
# relative path with no ISSUES token. Both arguments still hold; they are simply no
# longer load-bearing, because those checks now narrow the one set and inherit its
# filters. That is a strict improvement over an argued-safe asymmetry: an unexplained
# asymmetry is what the next person "fixes" into a bug, and an explained one still has
# to be re-argued every time a check is added.
#
# The exclusion is applied where the contract set is PRODUCED — once per port, in the
# one function each has for the job. That is the invariant to pin. Pinning the SHAPE of
# the two implementations would only create a new thing to drift, which is this
# contract's whole subject.
#
# maxdepth IS A TRIPWIRE, NOT A FILTER. Exceeding it is a HARD ERROR: both gates refuse
# to run and name every offending path. Raise the budget here — then re-diff
# `--list-scan` against `-ListScan` — or move the files.
#
# As a filter it was a silent coverage cliff, and the cliff is what made one shared set
# unsafe. A file past the budget was simply absent, and "absent because the budget
# dropped it" is indistinguishable from "absent because the tree does not contain it";
# binding every check to one enumeration would therefore have REDUCED what check 1
# caught, silently, which is the exact shape of the defects this file exists to close.
# Failing loudly inverts that: the budget stops being a cliff and becomes a statement
# that the tree has outgrown the contract.
#
# It is expressed in `find` terms, where `find <root> -maxdepth 1` means the root's
# immediate children. There is no longer a `Get-ChildItem -Depth` off-by-one conversion:
# both ports derive depth from the repo-relative PATH — segments below the root — rather
# than from the walker. That conversion used to be the one place the two implementations
# were licensed to differ, and this line once claimed `-SelfTest` asserted it, which the
# Windows seat found to be untrue: what existed was a comment about blast radius, not a
# check. Removing the difference is better than asserting it.
#
# WHAT `--self-test`/`-SelfTest` NOW ASSERTS, each assertion able to fail on its own and
# each mutation-tested against the defect it exists to catch:
#   - a file at exactly `maxdepth` is present in the scan set;
#   - a file at `maxdepth`+1 makes the gate FAIL and names the path — not merely "is
#     absent from the set", because absence is also what a broken enumerator produces;
#   - `--list-scan`'s output is byte-identical to the set the checks consume. That is
#     the assertion that would have caught the original defect, in which the parity
#     artifact printed one enumeration while the checks ran over another.
# `file` entries are named individually rather than reached by a walk, so they match no
# root prefix and are never depth-counted.

root src
root tests
root sdd
root scripts
root packaging
root gtktest
root data
# `probes/` holds measurement rigs and their README — C sources, a nested Rust
# workspace, and prose that cites the registers. It is enumerated for the same reason
# every other root is: a gate is its pattern AND its file set, and a directory silently
# outside the set is the exact drift this contract was written after. Depth 2 (the nested
# crate's src/ costs a segment), well inside the maxdepth tripwire. Parity with the
# PowerShell port proven by an identical ordinal-sorted list: 323 entries, 11 under
# probes/, SHA256 F663...40ED on both platforms.
root probes

file AGENTS.md
file README.md
file THIRD-PARTY-LICENSES.md
file Cargo.toml
file build.rs
# Both carry ANTI-PATTERNS citations in comments and neither was in the set, so the
# citation-form gate (check 8) could not see them — `.cargo/config.toml` is the
# process-wide `[env]` choke point and `clippy.toml` the method-ban list, which is
# exactly where a lesson gets cited. Named as `file` entries rather than adding a
# `root .cargo` so no dot-directory is walked. Found by grepping the WHOLE tracked
# tree for the banned form and comparing against what the gate reported: a gate is
# its pattern AND its set, and the two disagreed here in the set's direction.
file .cargo/config.toml
file clippy.toml

prescriptive sdd/POLICY.md
prescriptive sdd/TDD.md
prescriptive AGENTS.md
prescriptive README.md

exclude target/
exclude build/
exclude docs/
exclude .git/
exclude .agents/
exclude .claude/
exclude tests/reports/
# A nested crate's build tree, and the reason it needs its own line rather than being
# covered by `exclude target/` above: the exclusions are repo-relative path PREFIXES, and
# `probes/binding-shape-rs/target/` does not begin with `target/`. Adding `probes` as a
# root therefore brought that crate's build output into the set for anyone who has ever
# built the rig, and cargo's own artefacts go deeper than the maxdepth budget, so BOTH
# gates refuse to run and name a `.o` file. Measured by the macOS seat on a real
# `cargo build --manifest-path probes/binding-shape-rs/Cargo.toml`, and reproduced here
# by hand. It is the same gitignored-tree reasoning as every exclusion above — the
# verdict must depend on the commit, not on what the developer happened to build — and
# it changes no scan set on a clean checkout, where the directory does not exist, so
# the two ports' ordinal lists are untouched.
exclude probes/binding-shape-rs/target/

maxdepth 6
